    {
      "window_id": "2025.acl-long.1495::sections::6",
      "paper_id": "2025.acl-long.1495",
      "section_number": "6",
      "section_title": "experiments",
      "normalized_section_title": "experiments",
      "window_text": "We evaluate state-of-the-art LLMs on two core aspects of the DDxTutor framework: (1) the ability to generate comprehensive DDx reasoning chains that can serve as teaching references, and (2) the capacity to conduct tutoring dialogues that guide students through systematic diagnostic reasoning. For evaluation, we fine-tune three specialized groups of LLMs:\n\n(1) Teaching Reference Generator $(f_{1})$ : These models are trained to generate complete teaching references $\\hat{\\mathcal{T}}$ given the clinical case input $\\mathcal{I}$ : $\\hat{\\mathcal{T}} = f_{1}(\\mathcal{I})$ .   \n(2) Local Analysis Stage Dialogue Tutor $(f_{2})$ : These models $f_{2}$ are trained to generate teaching responses $t_{k}^{i}$ based on the clinical clue $c_{i}$ , history dialogue $D_{local,0:(k-1)}^{i}$ , student's last utterance $u_{k}^{i}$ , and local teaching reference $l_{i}$ : $t_{k}^{i} = f_{2}(c_{i},D_{local,0:(k-1)},u_{k}^{i},l_{i})$ .   \n(3) Global Synthesis Stage Dialogue Tutor $(f_{3})$ : These models $f_{3}$ generate final feedback $g_{1}$ based on all local analyses with aggregated information $HA$ , student's final diagnosis $p_{1}$ , and global teaching reference $\\mathbf{G}$ : $\\hat{g}_{1} = f_{3}(\\mathbf{L}, HA, p_{1}, \\mathbf{G})$ .\n\nWe select models with parameters ranging from 3B to 32B, including 7 general open source LLMs: Qwen2.5-3B, Qwen2.5-7B, Qwen 2.5-14B, Qwen 2.5-32B (Qwen et al., 2025), LLama3.2-3B, LLama3.1-8B (Grattafori et al., 2024), Phi4-14B (Abdin et al., 2024) and 2 Medical LLMs: Meditron-8B (Chen et al., 2023, 2024b), and Med42-8B (Christophe et al., 2024).",
