      "section_number": "D.4",
      "section_title": "validity of automatic evaluation metrics",
      "evidence": "Although BERTSCORE (Zhang et al., 2019) and METEOR (Banerjee and Lavie, 2005) are widely used in general NLP and medical-NLP researches (Huang et al., 2024; Yao et al., 2024a; Chen et al., 2024a). In this section, we further study how these semantic similarity scores could align with human judgment.\n\nExperimental setup. We randomly sampled 100 predictions produced by our models. Two doctors independently rated each prediction on a five-point Likert scale ( $1 =$ very poor, $5 =$ excellent), judging how well the generated texts matched the ground-truth answers from a medical perspective. We then computed Kendall's $\\tau$ between the doctors' scores and four common text-generation metrics—BLEU (Papineni et al., 2002), ROUGE-L (Lin, 2004), METEOR, and BERTSCORE.\n\nResults and discussion. Table 9 shows that ME-TEOR and BERTSCORE exhibit stronger correlation with expert judgment $(\\tau \\approx 0.4 - 0.5)$ than metrics such as BLEU and ROUGE-L. The magnitude of these correlation coefficients is comparable\n\nTable 8: Performance of Local Dialogue Mode $f_{2}$ and Global Dialogue Model $f_{3}$ after removing Teaching Reference $L$ and $G$ on the simulated dialogue datasets.   \n\n<table><tr><td>Model</td><td>IOUa</td><td>Acc_stance</td><td>Bert_reason</td><td>Acc_vs</td><td>Acc_vr</td><td>Bertfeed</td><td>Meteorfeed</td><td>Acc_og</td><td>Acc_vd</td><td>Bertfeed_g</td><td>Meteorfeed_g</td></tr><tr><td>Qwen2.5-3b</td><td>0.995</td><td>0.989</td><td>0.947</td><td>0.828</td><td>0.804</td><td>0.891</td><td>0.397</td><td>0.994</td><td>0.963</td><td>0.907</td><td>0.482</td></tr><tr><td>Llama3.2-3b</td><td>0.996</td><td>0.990</td><td>0.951</td><td>0.842</td><td>0.819</td><td>0.896</td><td>0.430</td><td>0.989</td><td>0.949</td><td>0.908</td><td>0.467</td></tr><tr><td>Qwen2.5-7b</td><td>0.994</td><td>0.990</td><td>0.947</td><td>0.844</td><td>0.812</td><td>0.892</td><td>0.408</td><td>0.889</td><td>0.969</td><td>0.906</td><td>0.477</td></tr><tr><td>Llama3.1-8b</td><td>0.997</td><td>0.992</td><td>0.953</td><td>0.863</td><td>0.835</td><td>0.898</td><td>0.446</td><td>0.997</td><td>0.975</td><td>0.909</td><td>0.473</td></tr><tr><td>Meditron-8b</td><td>0.996</td><td>0.991</td><td>0.953</td><td>0.853</td><td>0.830</td><td>0.898</td><td>0.442</td><td>0.997</td><td>0.972</td><td>0.909</td><td>0.475</td></tr><tr><td>Med42-8b</td><td>0.997</td><td>0.993</td><td>0.955</td><td>0.868</td><td>0.838</td><td>0.899</td><td>0.456</td><td>0.989</td><td>0.966</td><td>0.909</td><td>0.475</td></tr><tr><td>Qwen2.5-14b</td><td>0.982</td><td>0.991</td><td>0.948</td><td>0.866</td><td>0.836</td><td>0.894</td><td>0.421</td><td>0.966</td><td>0.980</td><td>0.909</td><td>0.490</td></tr><tr><td>Phi4-14b</td><td>0.997</td><td>0.993</td><td>0.955</td><td>0.888</td><td>0.857</td><td>0.901</td><td>0.468</td><td>0.997</td><td>0.989</td><td>0.912</td><td>0.484</td></tr><tr><td>Qwen2.5-32b</td><td>0.996</td><td>0.993</td><td>0.950</td><td>0.878</td><td>0.846</td><td>0.897</td><td>0.437</td><td>0.997</td><td>0.980</td><td>0.911</td><td>0.498</td></tr></table>\n\nTable 9: Kendall $\\tau$ correlations between automatic metrics and human ratings. $^{**}p <   0.01$ $^{***}p <   0.001$   \n\n<table><tr><td>Human\\Metric</td><td>BLEU</td><td>ROUGE-L</td><td>METEOR</td><td>BERTScore</td><td>D1/D2</td></tr><tr><td>D1</td><td>0.39***</td><td>0.45***</td><td>0.49***</td><td>0.50***</td><td>0.57***</td></tr><tr><td>D2</td><td>0.26**</td><td>0.37***</td><td>0.39***</td><td>0.41***</td><td>0.57***</td></tr></table>\n\nto what (Zhang et al., 2019) reported for machine-translation benchmarks, supporting the appropriateness of using the two semantic metrics in our specialized scenario.\n\nAs METEOR and BERTSCORE align more closely and consistently with doctors' assessments, we retain them as the primary automatic semantic metrics for our experiments."
